  already logs [38;5;153musername[39m and returns [38;5;153m{ success: true, user: username }[39m, so "who logged in" is arguably already tracked by
  username.

  [1mThe blocker:[0m nothing in this repo produces a [38;5;153muserId[39m. [38;5;153mindex.html[39m collects only [38;5;153musername[39m and [38;5;153mpassword[39m. So adding the
  parameter means deciding who supplies it — and the three answers lead to genuinely different work.

  - [1mServer returns it[0m — the login response carries the id; you don't pass it [3min[0m, you read it [3mout[0m. That means no new
    parameter at all: [38;5;153mlogin()[39m's return shape changes instead. Cheapest, matches how ids normally work, but it's not the
    change you asked for.
  - [1mCaller supplies it from the form[0m — add a [38;5;153muserId[39m input to [38;5;153mindex.html[39m and thread it through. Does exactly what you
    asked, but it changes the UI and asks users to type an id they probably don't know. Hard to walk back once the form
    ships.
  - [1mClient-generated correlation id[0m — generate an id in the client and pass it as [38;5;153muserId[39m for tracing a login attempt. No
    UI change, but it identifies a [3msession[0m, not a person — so it won't answer "who logged in."

  My recommendation is the first: the server is the only thing that actually knows a user's id, and reading it off the
  response avoids adding a parameter the caller can't honestly fill. But that depends on what "track" means to you — a
  log line, an analytics event, or something stored.

  Two things I'd also flag regardless of which you pick: a third parameter changes [38;5;153mlogin()[39m's signature, so the call site
  at [38;5;153mapp.js:23[39m must change with it; and [38;5;153mlogin()[39m is a stub that never calls [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m, so any "tracking" is a
  [38;5;153mconsole.log[39m until that's real.
